   \\[\n   f(-2) = 3\n   \\]\n\n2. **Express the new function in terms of the original function:**\n   The new function is \\( y = f(2x+1) + 3 \\). We need to find a point \\((a, b)\\) such that this point lies on the graph of the new function.\n\n3. **Set up the equation for the new function:**\n   If \\((a, b)\\) is on the graph of \\( y = f(2x+1) + 3 \\), then:\n   \\[\n   b = f(2a+1) + 3\n   \\]\n\n4. **Relate the new function to the original function:**\n   We know from the given point that \\( f(-2) = 3 \\). We need to find \\( a \\) such that \\( 2a+1 = -2 \\).\n\n5. **Solve for \\( a \\):**\n   \\[\n   2a + 1 = -2\n   \\]\n   Subtract 1 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   2a = -3\n   \\]\n   Divide by 2:\n   \\[\n   a = -\\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Find \\( b \\) using the value of \\( a \\):**\n   Substitute \\( a = -\\frac{3}{2} \\) into the equation \\( b = f(2a+1) + 3 \\):\n   \\[\n   b = f\\left(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) + 1\\right) + 3\n   \\]\n   Simplify inside the function:\n   \\[\n   b = f(-3 + 1) + 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = f(-2) + 3\n   \\]\n   Since \\( f(-2) = 3 \\):\n   \\[\n   b = 3 + 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = 6\n   \\]\n\n7. **Write the final answer:**\n   The point \\((a, b)\\) on the graph of \\( y = f(2x+1) + 3 \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\left( -\\frac{3}{2}, 6 \\right)\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left( -\\frac{3}{2}, 6 \\right)}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\left( -\\frac{3}{2}, 6 \\right)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:735"}
